Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential use  
• trees.zipcode : join‑key that links the tree census to the income table  
• trees.boroname : borough label we need to aggregate on  
• income_trees.zipcode : join‑key that carries the income information  
• income_trees.Estimate_Median_income : used to keep only rows whose median income > 0  
• income_trees.Estimate_Mean_income : averaged for each borough (must also be > 0 in our filter)

Main conditions that will be applied later  
• trees.boroname IS NOT NULL AND trees.boroname <> ''  
• income_trees.Estimate_Median_income  > 0  
• income_trees.Estimate_Mean_income    > 0  
• trees.zipcode = income_trees.zipcode  

Below are ten exploratory → final SQL queries (all limited to 20 rows) that progressively build toward answering the task.  

```sql
-- 1. Peek at a handful of tree records, including borough and ZIP code
SELECT idx, tree_id, zipcode, boroname 
FROM trees
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Inspect some income rows so we can see the income columns by ZIP
SELECT zipcode,
       Estimate_Median_income,
       Estimate_Mean_income
FROM income_trees
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. List distinct ZIP codes that appear in the tree data (with a borough name)
SELECT DISTINCT zipcode
FROM trees
WHERE boroname IS NOT NULL AND boroname <> ''
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. List distinct ZIP codes that appear in the income table
SELECT DISTINCT zipcode
FROM income_trees
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Try a quick inner‑join of both tables on ZIP to make sure the key works
SELECT t.idx,
       t.boroname,
       t.zipcode,
       i.Estimate_Median_income,
       i.Estimate_Mean_income
FROM trees            AS t
JOIN income_trees     AS i
  ON t.zipcode = i.zipcode
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Same join but keep only rows whose median & mean income are positive
SELECT t.idx,
       t.boroname,
       t.zipcode,
       i.Estimate_Median_income,
       i.Estimate_Mean_income
FROM trees        AS t
JOIN income_trees AS i
  ON t.zipcode = i.zipcode
WHERE i.Estimate_Median_income > 0
  AND i.Estimate_Mean_income   > 0
  AND t.boroname IS NOT NULL 
  AND t.boroname <> ''
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Count how many tree records each borough has after the join + filters
SELECT t.boroname,
       COUNT(*) AS tree_count
FROM trees        AS t
JOIN income_trees AS i
  ON t.zipcode = i.zipcode
WHERE i.Estimate_Median_income > 0
  AND i.Estimate_Mean_income   > 0
  AND t.boroname IS NOT NULL 
  AND t.boroname <> ''
GROUP BY t.boroname
ORDER BY tree_count DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Compute the average mean income for every borough (still with filters)
SELECT t.boroname,
       AVG(i.Estimate_Mean_income) AS avg_mean_income
FROM trees        AS t
JOIN income_trees AS i
  ON t.zipcode = i.zipcode
WHERE i.Estimate_Median_income > 0
  AND i.Estimate_Mean_income   > 0
  AND t.boroname IS NOT NULL 
  AND t.boroname <> ''
GROUP BY t.boroname
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Put the tree count and average mean income together, order by count
SELECT t.boroname,
       COUNT(*)                       AS tree_count,
       AVG(i.Estimate_Mean_income)    AS avg_mean_income
FROM trees        AS t
JOIN income_trees AS i
  ON t.zipcode = i.zipcode
WHERE i.Estimate_Median_income > 0
  AND i.Estimate_Mean_income   > 0
  AND t.boroname IS NOT NULL 
  AND t.boroname <> ''
GROUP BY t.boroname
ORDER BY tree_count DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL QUERY – top 3 boroughs with most trees + their avg mean income
SELECT t.boroname                             AS borough,
       COUNT(*)                               AS total_trees,
       AVG(i.Estimate_Mean_income)            AS avg_mean_income
FROM trees        AS t
JOIN income_trees AS i
  ON t.zipcode = i.zipcode
WHERE i.Estimate_Median_income > 0
  AND i.Estimate_Mean_income   > 0
  AND t.boroname IS NOT NULL 
  AND t.boroname <> ''
GROUP BY t.boroname
ORDER BY total_trees DESC
LIMIT 3;
```

Running the last statement will return the three boroughs that host the greatest number of trees (after the specified filters) together with the corresponding average mean income for each borough.
